  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "Switch off the power. An SSD still remembers your files. Inside each flash cell, a special region can trap electrical charge. The amount of charge changes the cell's threshold voltage. To read it, the controller applies a test voltage and checks whether the cell conducts. That response reveals the stored value. Billions of cells are organized into pages and blocks. Writing gradually wears the insulating material that holds the charge. So, the controller spreads new writes across different cells, a process called wear-leveling. Trapped charge preserves the data while careful reading and wear-leveling keep the SSD useful.",
